WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:13.090
This is what you get when
you open up the UI editors is

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.190
kind of like the base screen.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.730
And so we have three
columns going left to right.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.010
And so the Elements panel has
all of the various different UI

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.470
components that you
can put in your app

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.240
that you can control
with the UI editor.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.670
So we have Action Button,
Numeric Inputs, Plot Outputs,

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:37.810
Cards, and Plots and stuff
like that, everything

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.280
that you would need to
make a basic app, more

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.810
of which are coming soon.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.450
So then moving over to the
right, we have the app view.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.510
And this isn't like an
exact view of your app.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.150
It's more of an
abstract representation

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.780
of the app with the
intent of basically

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.400
being a visual
representation of the code.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.140
And the reason why is that a
lot of times, your actual app

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.722
may have stuff like
dynamic UI in it.

00:01:07.722 --> 00:01:09.680
And so we can't know what
that would look like.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.710
So this is basically
a way of, we're

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.780
going to put things
in the right places,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:17.180
and we're going to give you
the pertinent information.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.620
But if you think of it kind
of as a wireframe of your app.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.950
And then to the right of that,
we have the Properties panel.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.380
And so if we select a given
UI component in the app editor

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.590
view, on the right hand side,
you get to see the properties.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.410
And so you see where
that given component

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.670
is in the tree of your UI.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.090
So you have the Grid Page.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.310
Within that, you
have the Grid Card.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.980
And within that,
you have the Slider.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.960
And then you have
the various settings

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.100
like Input ID, Label, the
values that you can change.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.840
And all of these,
you change them here.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.810
And then it will
update the app for you.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.030
You can delete that element
down with this button.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.215
And then beneath that, there's
a thing called the App Preview.

00:02:04.215 --> 00:02:05.590
And so this goes
back to the fact

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.259
that this isn't a perfect
representation of your app.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.690
You see this is not a real plot.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.000
This just kind of
an abstract plot.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.990
But down here, whenever
you run the UI editor,

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.880
you get a preview of the app.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.580
And so this is what the app
looks like actually running.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.230
And as you change
stuff within your app,

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.310
it will update down there.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:37.351
So if we change the app title
content to be "Shiny UI Editor"

00:02:37.351 --> 00:02:40.250
and we go back here, we
see that it changed here.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.780
And so you never have to
turn off the UI editor

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.760
and restart your app to
see what it looks like.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.020
We'll do that
automatically for you.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.160
We'll also give you the
logs that your app puts out.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:54.790
You can refresh the page
in case there's something

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:58.090
that changed that
needs a page refresh.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.920
So, yeah, that's the
very basic set of tools.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.248
And then there's
this panel up here.

00:03:06.248 --> 00:03:07.540
It has a little bit of control.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.480
So, say, we do something
like, take this, move it down

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.470
here, and then move our slider
up to a new card over here.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.898
We say "newslider."

00:03:16.898 --> 00:03:18.190
And then we say, wait a minute.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.050
I don't actually like that.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.680
We can use the Back button
up here to undo that change,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.020
go back all the way
to where we were.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.070
Maybe we did like it.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.800
We can redo.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.010
But let's go back to that.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.410
So, basically, these
Undo and Redo buttons

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.470
are there to save
you if you ever

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:38.720
get yourself into a situation
that you don't want to be in.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.380
You can always just
back up until your app

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:44.157
is working and looking the
way that you want it to.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.865
So we talked about
the element deletions.

00:03:45.865 --> 00:03:48.700
Let's say we want to delete
this plot called bluePlot.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.500
We you can just click
the Delete Element.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.410
You can see down
there, it's gone.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.030
But once, again,
let's put it back.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:59.660
Then we can do things like
resize the given component.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.710
So, say, right now, we
have the slider on the left

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.900
and the plots on the right.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.297
And then the plot on
the top is the same size

00:04:07.297 --> 00:04:08.380
as the plot on the bottom.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.930
Maybe we want to make
the plot on the top

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.339
be maybe a little bit
more of the given size.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.300
So we can just drag this
little sizing down here.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.890
And you see the size of
this pop up next to stuff.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.730
And it uses this thing
called an fr, which

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:26.980
we'll talk about a little bit.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.960
But that's the CSS unit.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.120
So, basically, it's
a ratio of the sizes.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.050
So we're going to say for every
0.5 units of the bottom plot,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:36.530
the top plot is 1.5.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.530
So it's going to be
three times the size.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.570
We can stop there
and go back and see.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.740
Yep, it made that change.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:46.750
And so, basically, we can
use this to fiddle around

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:47.950
with the sizes.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:52.628
Say, we're dragging this, and
we see it's 200 or 305 pixels.

00:04:52.628 --> 00:04:54.670
And, say, we really wanted
it to actually be 300.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.740
We couldn't get our
drag to be there.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.420
We can mouse over that and
just go over here and type 300

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.770
and update it that way as well.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.103
I showed it a little bit before.

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:08.550
But you can resize these
panels within this grid

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.680
by using these little handles.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.860
And then you can drag them
down so-- with our settings

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.600
all the way down there.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.340
If you wanted to, maybe we use
that to make this plot bigger.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.280
And, yeah, you can
configure everything

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.970
to your heart's
desire, just using

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.850
underneath the hood a package
called Grid Layout, which

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.630
we are releasing in conjunction
with Shiny UI editor,

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:39.540
which allows you to make your
apps laid out very explicitly

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:40.920
with grids.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.490
So when you actually look--

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.270
I guess we can talk
about that later.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.400
But we just defined where
we want things to be,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.240
how big the given elements,
the tracks of the grid

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.010
are, the rows and
columns of the grid.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:00.090
And then we can just put an
element over whatever span

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.842
of contiguous rows and
columns that we want.

00:06:02.842 --> 00:06:05.050
And we know that it's going
to be placed right there.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.950
We don't have to work with
arbitrary restrictions

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.488
such as, we can
only have columns,

00:06:09.488 --> 00:06:10.530
or we can only have rows.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.610
You can have both.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:12.990
Basically, if you
can imagine it,

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.030
you can put it into
the grid layout.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.320
We can just add a bunch
of columns if we want.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.680
And we do that by going over to
these individual column views.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.710
And then you see there's a
little plus button over here.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:26.610
And you mouse over.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.600
It says, add a column.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.950
So we can do that.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:32.140
And then we get as
many as we want.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.630
And so right now, we
have this name all

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:37.650
scrunched up over there.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:41.850
But we could make it
three of the column's--

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.990
or whatever size we want to.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.320
We can resize-- let's see.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.540
Let's add a bunch
of rows as well.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.430
I have to go up here.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.320
And we're getting
a little bit crazy.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.030
But we can make that there.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:01.650
And then we can take our
second plot, make it that.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.130
We have this kind of
crazy grid structure.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:10.507
And what we could do is, say,
let's put a text input here.

00:07:10.507 --> 00:07:12.090
So we can drag a
[? textbook ?] input.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:18.130
We're going to call it namefield
or something like that.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.200
Put it that there.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:22.700
You can drag this bigger.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.260
We could just keep
going and building

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:32.340
all sorts of arbitrary
sizings, title up here.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.385
Maybe we want to put our slider
all the way up here, probably

00:07:36.385 --> 00:07:38.010
drag this down a
little bit, so there's

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.860
a little bit more space for it.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:47.640
Basically, the world is
your oyster, so to speak,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.380
with what you put in this stuff.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:55.830
So, yeah, we can
build any design

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:59.790
that you might possibly
want as long as it

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:03.370
can be expressed as a grid.

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:04.410
You can put it on here.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:09.903
In Excel, if you want to
add a column between things,

00:08:09.903 --> 00:08:11.820
you just kind of mouse
up in this little area,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.510
right click here.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.427
You don't right click.

00:08:13.427 --> 00:08:14.160
You just hover.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.500
And it pops up this
little interface.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:19.740
You can also delete rows.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.150
And so it will
intelligently say,

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.650
hey, you can delete this track
because everything that's

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:26.920
within it won't disappear.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.600
So we can delete that.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.740
But, say, we tried
to delete this tract.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.039
It won't let us.

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:36.630
And that's because
if we delete it,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.549
this whole element wouldn't
have nowhere to go.

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:43.230
And so you can simplify your--

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.750
maybe you get your layout
working the way you want to.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:51.098
You can then start taking out
the various tracks to simplify.

00:08:51.098 --> 00:08:52.140
Or you can leave them in.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.420
It doesn't really matter.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:09:00.540
You'll see that the code
generated for grid layout,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.970
it's easier to read if
you have a simpler layout.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:07.600
But if you don't, it
doesn't bother you,

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.770
you can have as many columns
and rows as you possibly want.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:17.490
And you can make things look
exactly how you want them to.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:21.750
Dynamic UI Output is
very literally just

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.500
like outputUI, renderUI.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.610
Grid Text Card is
when you just want

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.180
to have place some
text in the grid.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:40.560
And so you see here, this title
here is just a grid text card.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:46.200
And so this is just a
component that you just

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.370
want to put text somewhere.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.380
You put it in this.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:53.220
And so it's usually used for
a title of your app, maybe

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.630
a footer.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:58.740
It's a pretty simple function.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:03.420
Grid Plot Card is
somewhat-- so see here,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.400
this is a grid plot card.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.450
And that can be put
directly onto the grid page.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.690
And it's basically just like
a shortcut for having a card.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.160
So this is a grid card.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.090
If you were to have a grid
card and then put a plot in it,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.440
it's essentially the same thing.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.900
But a lot of times,
you just want

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.370
to put a plot in a given place.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.110
A grid plot card does that.

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.980
And it takes away some of
the annoying details of--

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.235
you have to-- if you've ever
put a plot in a Shiny app, which

00:10:32.235 --> 00:10:33.735
I would assume a
lot of people have,

00:10:33.735 --> 00:10:36.720
you know that it starts
at 400 pixels high.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.970
And so almost
always, you're going

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.760
to want to change that
to some other value.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.760
And grid plot card
is smart about that

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.950
and knows that the grid
is going to give you

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.230
sizing of your plot for you.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:51.900
It will set those
height and width

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.840
to automatically fill the
card space that you have.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:01.050
And so that's nice, just allows
you to skip some of the syntax.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.630
And instead of having to have a
card and then put a plot output

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:06.630
in there, then make sure
that the plot output is

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:10.870
the right sizing, you can just
drag grid plot card on there.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:16.810
And so you could say, right,
how we have distPlot down here.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:22.050
So if we delete distPlot
and we put a plain card here

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.988
and we call it distPlot--

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:32.745
and then we take a
plot output right here

00:11:32.745 --> 00:11:34.020
and drag it in there.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:41.790
So this plot output needs to
have distPlot set, distPlot.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.310
And you see that the
height is 400 pixels.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.344
We want to set this to 100%.

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:52.860
And then that's effectively
the exact same thing

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:57.060
that we get just for
free with grid plot card.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.420
Another nice thing is that you
notice that all of these cards

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:04.920
have to have these names when
we drag something new into here.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.330
It says you need to name this.

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:07.830
And that's because
grid layout needs

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:11.130
to have some string to identify
the given areas that you've

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.057
defined in the grid.

00:12:13.057 --> 00:12:15.390
And a lot of times, if you're
going to put a plot there,

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:17.670
you probably are
making that plot area

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.210
the exact same
string as the plot

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.190
ID that you're using
to hook up your plot.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:29.580
And so you can get that really
easily with grid plot card.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:30.960
You put it down there.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.230
And we just call this distPlot.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.430
Say Done.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:38.847
It automatically hooks it up
to that distplot argument.

00:12:38.847 --> 00:12:39.930
You don't have to do that.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:43.320
You see in the settings, name
of grid area is distPlot.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:44.820
And then it
automatically does that.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:46.487
But we could put a
different output idea

00:12:46.487 --> 00:12:48.060
if we wanted to,
call it distPlot2.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:51.150
It's going to break because
distPlot2 doesn't exist.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:56.020
But the idea is that we give
as nice a default as you

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:58.260
could want for your plot.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.810
And then if you
need more control,

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.690
you would just put a
plot in a grid card.

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:08.430
So you'll notice when we
put an element-- so, say,

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.130
we are going to drag--

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:15.330
the child of the
grid layout needs

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:17.880
to be a grid card of some kind.

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.450
We can directly put the
grid plot card there

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.100
because that's a grid card.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.600
So it's a direct child.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:28.500
But if we take something
like a numeric input,

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:31.080
like we had before,
and we drag it here,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.323
it says you can
put it on the grid.

00:13:33.323 --> 00:13:34.740
When we put it on
the grid, that's

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.540
going to ask us the
name of this grid area.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:39.977
We could say [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:39.977 --> 00:13:41.560
But then if you
actually look at this,

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.430
you'll see that it
wrapped it in a grid card.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:46.680
And so this is a full card.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.410
And you can put more stuff
in here if you wanted.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:50.070
It depends.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:54.600
I think that's up to
you, the app builder.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:59.730
If you want to start
with placing your cards,

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:11.100
you could basically empty your
whole app, down to just a grid,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.630
just super simple.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.410
You could say, OK, I
want a bunch of columns,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.460
and I want them all
to be the same size.

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:22.530
we'll make this 1 fr.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.697
So everything is going to be--

00:14:27.439 --> 00:14:31.730
you need evenly sized,
although this is 1.5.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:33.035
So we'll change it to 1.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:38.170
So now we just have a grid.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.480
And then you could
just take your cards

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.310
and put them on there.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:44.870
Call this plotA.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:49.250
Position it around.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:50.840
And you can just
continue to do that.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:54.410
PlotB.

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:02.880
Title headers like that.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.630
If you wanted to, you could
continue to do that and then

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:07.740
place your stuff on there.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.420
And, honestly, if
you wanted to, you

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:13.260
could just use the UI editor
to build this, get your layout,

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:15.180
and then go into
the code, and add

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:20.520
your stuff within those
little built-out skeleton.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:25.110
So we're agnostic about
which way you do it.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.930
Sometimes you might want the
control of putting the grid

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:28.620
card directly.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:30.960
But know that any time you
put an item that is not

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:34.937
a grid-aware item, so it's
not a grid card on it,

00:15:34.937 --> 00:15:37.020
it's going to just wrap
it in a grid card for you.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:42.180
So you can save a step
if you want but maybe you

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:44.800
want to put titles here.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:49.470
So let's say, like,
plot A. And then

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:51.510
you'll get a title
for your card first.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:54.900
And so maybe you want
to set it up that way

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.390
instead of going back
and retroactively

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.850
assigning the settings
for your given card.

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:03.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

